1. What is inheritance?
Inheritance is a feature in Java that allows one class to acquire accessible fields and methods from another class.

It helps us reuse existing code instead of writing the same functionality again.

For example, imagine you are building an e-commerce application. You have a User class containing a name and an email.
 You want to create Customer and Admin classes.

Both customers and admins have names and email addresses,
so you can define those common features in the User class and inherit them in the other classes.



Types of inheritance in Java
Java supports these main forms of class inheritance:


1. Single inheritance
One child inherits from one parent.
class Car extends Vehicle

2. Multilevel inheritance
A class inherits from a child class, creating multiple levels.
Animal → Dog → Puppy

3. Hierarchical inheritance
Multiple child classes inherit from one parent.
Vehicle → Car, Bike

4. Multiple inheritance
A class inherits from multiple parents. Java does not support this through classes, but a class can implement multiple interfaces.
class SmartPhone implements Camera, GPS